        'For high earners in their peak earning years, traditional may be better. You get a significant tax deduction at your current high marginal rate, and you may be in a lower bracket in retirement when you withdraw and pay taxes.',
        'If you are unsure about your future tax situation, consider diversifying by having both traditional and Roth accounts. This gives you flexibility in retirement to withdraw from either account depending on your tax situation each year.',
        'Consider state taxes as well. If you live in a high-tax state now but plan to retire in a state with no income tax, traditional may be more advantageous, as you avoid state taxes on contributions and withdraw in a tax-free state.',
